Report where IOKit stops through the boot-progress panel, not printf, and keep the root nub out of the type zones
(judyp bring-up).

Two rounds of printfs placed after PE_init_iokit printed nothing, and this round's photograph shows why that is
not evidence about IOKit. The panel's "Kernel console" section still ends on

  Serial requested, consistent debug disabled or debug boot arg not present, confi...

which osfmk/kdp/kdp_udp.c prints from kdp_serial_init during machine_startup -- that is, before PE_init_iokit runs.
PE_init_iokit's first statement is PE_init_printf(TRUE), i.e. vcattach(), and from there the console belongs to the
video console: vcattach() calls initialize_screen(NULL, kPEAcquireScreen) and replays msgbuf through vcputc().
Nothing an IOKit printf or IOLog writes afterwards reaches the panel, so the six lines the previous round added to
InitIOKit and the three in PE_lockdown_iokit could not have appeared even if the boot had passed them. What the same
frame does say is unchanged: the stage row names PE_init_iokit (startup recording) and 1188562 pages are free, so the
boot stops inside PE_init_iokit, with the interrupt mask set and no panic.

So the instrumentation moves to the one channel that does not go through printf: package/BootProgress.c's
boot_progress_stage(), which records the text as a stage and then paints the framebuffer from the caller while the
monitor thread has not run (started && !thread_count). That paint happens synchronously at the breadcrumb, so it
survives the IRQ-off hard lock the panel's own heading warns about, and the page already renders the last six stages:
one photograph names the last step reached.

Three changes:

  1. BC01..BC22 walk PE_init_iokit -> InitIOKit -> ConfigureIOKit and BC30..BC33 walk startIOServiceMatching, one
     per step, so a stop is a step name instead of a function name; BC40..BC42 keep bracketing PE_lockdown_iokit.

  2. "NATIVE_CTL: PE_init_iokit entered" is printed immediately before PE_init_printf(TRUE). It is the only printf
     added, and it is the control that separates "the console dies at that statement" from "the boot never got
     there" without another round.

  3. OSObject_typed_operator_new still takes every OSObject from kheap_alloc instead of a kalloc_type zone
     (unchanged, still the one behavioural change). If the boot now reaches BC17 the type-zone allocation inside
     "new IOPlatformExpertDevice" is not the fault; if it stops between BC16 and BC17, it is.

The previous round's zalloc trace is dropped: it printed through the same dead console.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp
index d0149a6..dfedeb0 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOPlatformExpert.cpp
@@ -2086,15 +2086,24 @@ IOPlatformExpertDevice::init(void *dtRoot)
 	return true;
 }
 
+/* UNI_BC: see the comment in iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp -- the boot-progress panel paints from
+ * the caller, printf/IOLog no longer reach the screen after PE_init_printf(TRUE). */
+extern "C" void boot_progress_stage(const char *text) __attribute__((weak_import));
+#define UNI_BC(text) do { if (boot_progress_stage) boot_progress_stage(text); } while (0)
+
 bool
 IOPlatformExpertDevice::startIOServiceMatching(void)
 {
+	UNI_BC("BC30 startIOServiceMatching enter");
 	workLoop = IOWorkLoop::workLoop();
+	UNI_BC("BC31 workLoop allocated");
 	if (!workLoop) {
 		return false;
 	}
 
+	UNI_BC("BC32 registerService enter");
 	registerService();
+	UNI_BC("BC33 registerService done");
 
 	return true;
 }
diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
index d74c063..a11c591 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
@@ -130,6 +130,15 @@ iokit_post_constructor_init(void)
  */
 void (*record_startup_extensions_function)(void) = NULL;
 
+/*
+ * UNI_BC: package/BootProgress.c records the text as a stage and paints the framebuffer from the
+ * caller while the monitor thread has not run, so the last breadcrumb survives a hard lock. IOLog is
+ * not usable here: PE_init_iokit has already called PE_init_printf(TRUE), which points PE_putc at
